**Read:** [Radiative Transfer — MIT 2.58J](../sources/Radiative-Transfer-MIT-2-58J.lingua#r_0SGHEYZJFKN0ZNS3AYJ39XPT86), complete fluctuation–dissipation and near-field lectures; [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), relevant full wave/statistical transport sequence; [Nanoscale thermal transport II — Cahill and colleagues](../sources/Nanoscale-Thermal-Transport-II-Cahill.lingua#r_30EZYRYJ20TDTWN3JXWD9KWSFG), complete review and a primary near-field study.

Reconstruct the electromagnetic/statistical assumptions behind a near-field thermal-transfer model using the complete relevant MIT lectures and primary research. Specify materials, optical response, separation, temperatures and the meaning of the measured or calculated flux.

Compare with the far-field limit of the same configuration. Explain why a blackbody far-field bound is not the same statement as a near-field bound, and identify the roles of evanescent fields and dissipation. Test numerical integration, response-function conventions and small-gap material-model limits.

Deliver a reproducible theoretical comparison and a critical reading of a complete experiment. Examine spacing uncertainty, roughness, parasitic conduction and temperature inference. Do not equate a theoretical enhancement with a demonstrated useful device; assess the actual geometry, supported operating interval and unresolved loss mechanisms.

